# ontology

The **type layer** of this instance: every `.ent` at the root of this
repository, read together as one graph. `{{ontology-ent}}` (types, links,
actions) and `{{transform-ent}}` (sources, datasets) hold what no single project
owns; a project may have texts of its own beside them.
This checkout is for READING. A `.ent` text changes through the CLI —
`sutra projection pull` → edit → `projection push --preview` → `push` →
`commit` — never through `git push`; `AGENTS.md` has the exact commands and
`AUTHORING.md` how to work without stumbling. git carries only Python, in the
code repositories.

This README is a crash course. It is seeded once and then yours — edit it.

---

## 1. What a push does

`projection push <text>` sends exactly the checkout one `pull` wrote, merged
element by element over the base of that pull: what only others changed since
stays, what both changed differently is a conflict (HTTP 409, named per
element — decide with `--mine` or `--theirs`). A node the projection showed and
you left out is removed; a node it never showed is never touched.

`--preview` plans the push and stages nothing — use it every time. A refused
push stages nothing either, and names each problem. A green push stages on the
branch you named; `sutra --branch <b> commit` lands it there, and a proposal
carries it to `main`.

---

## 2. This tree is AUTHORITATIVE

The authored files TOGETHER are the WHOLE layer. A type you leave out of all
of them is a type you are asking to remove.

Which files are authored, in which language, and who owns them is itself
declared — by the projection `:ontologie` in the `:projections` section (here
in `{{ontology-ent}}`); its `:filename` lists the kinds of text it consists of:

```clojure
:projections
{:ontologie
 {:filename
  {:zentral   {:language :edn :envelope [:schema] :name ["ontology.ent"] :home-for [:*]}
   :transform {:language :edn :envelope [:schema] :name ["transform.ent"] :home-for [:sources :datasets]}
   :objekte   {:language :edn :envelope [:schema] :name ["*.objects.ent"] :owner [:declared :facet] :facet "objects"
               :sections [:projects :objects :links :interfaces :shared-properties :object-type-groups :actions]
               :home-for [:projects :objects :links :interfaces :shared-properties :object-type-groups :actions]}
   :ingest    {:language :edn :envelope [:schema] :name ["*.ingest.ent"] :owner [:declared :facet] :facet "ingest"
               :sections [:projects :sources :datasets] :home-for [:sources :datasets]}
   :apps      {:language :edn :envelope [:schema] :name ["*.apps.ent"] :owner [:declared :facet] :facet "apps"
               :sections [:projects :workspaces :collections :custom-applications :agents :object-sets]
               :home-for [:workspaces :collections :custom-applications :agents :object-sets]}
   ;; …and the generic *.ent / *.ent.yaml kinds: a project as one text of its own
   }}}
```

A project is written as up to three texts beside `{{ontology-ent}}`:
`<project>.objects.ent` (the project itself, its types, links and the actions on them),
`<project>.ingest.ent` (its sources and datasets), `<project>.apps.ent` (its
workspaces, applications, agents, object sets). What no project owns — value types, and
resources without a `:project` — stays in `{{ontology-ent}}`.

- `:name` — patterns over paths. When several match a path, only the most
  specific one reads it (`*.apps.ent` before `*.ent`): a text's NAME decides its
  kind, never its content. `:envelope` — where the schema body sits (`[:schema]`,
  or nothing: the file IS the body).
- `:owner` — how a text's project is found: `:declared` (it names one under
  `:projects`), `:file-name` (`werkzeug.ent`), `:facet` (`werkzeug.apps.ent`). An
  owning text's resources need no `:project`; a type that exists elsewhere is
  imported, not declared again, and an attribute a project adds to it is that
  project's.
- `:sections` — what such a text may hold; anything else in it is refused.
- `:home-for` — where a NEW resource of a section goes. On a central kind it is
  that literal file; on a project kind (one with `:owner`) it is the kind's
  pattern with the project in the wildcard — a new action of `werkzeug` goes to
  `werkzeug.objects.ent`. A resource already written somewhere stays there as long
  as its kind still takes it.
- The kinds are checked as a whole: a home outside its `:sections`, two project
  homes for one section, a project kind holding what no project owns, or two
  kinds on one pattern that disagree are refused before anything is read.
- A push is read with the kinds IT declares — change `:filename` and use it in
  the same push.
- One type may be split across texts of one project, as long as no two texts
  say different things about the same field or attribute. A file no kind of text
  reads is refused by name. Each check reads ALL files as one graph: a link may
  cross files and projects.
- This tree is a rendered READ copy. Where each resource, field and attribute
  lives is the projection's own placement on the branch, not this tree and not
  the schema: move a resource by cutting it from one text and pasting it into
  another — both texts pulled in one `sutra projection pull` — and pushing; the
  move needs no branch and takes effect at once — a placement is not the schema. `projection pull … --rehome` puts every resource at its home.

So: **pull again after every commit.** A stale base turns others' changes
into conflicts and shows errors that have nothing to do with your edit.

