## 3. The kinds of type, and when to reach for each

| kind | it is | reach for it when |
|---|---|---|
| **object type** | a business noun with identity (a primary key) | you can point at one and say *this order*, *this invoice* |
| **link type** | an edge between two object types | two nouns relate and you want to traverse it |
| **interface** | a CONTRACT several object types implement | see below — the one people over-use |
| **shared property** | one property DEFINITION reused across types | the same real column means the same thing everywhere (`:created-at`) |
| **value type** | a constrained scalar (format, regex, range) | the CONSTRAINT is the point, not the column (`:iban`, `:percent`) |
| **object-type group** | a label for grouping types | purely for navigation |
| **dataset** | a versioned table BELOW the ontology | the curated layer your object types are built on — §4.1 |
| **source** | a connector to a system outside | you need rows from an ERP, an inbox, an API |
| **action** | an authored write into the ontology | a user or a rule must be able to change data |

### When is it an interface?

An interface is a **contract**, not code reuse. Use one when several object
types genuinely answer the same question and something needs to ask that
question WITHOUT knowing which type it got:

- a polymorphic link (`:to-interface`) — one edge pointing at `:document`,
  satisfied by invoices, delivery notes and orders alike;
- a search or a view over the shape rather than over the type;
- a property set several types must all carry, enforced rather than hoped for.

It gives you two things: inherited **properties** (`:property-list`) and **link
obligations** (`:link-constraints`, which a concrete link fulfils via
`:satisfies`).

It is the WRONG tool when the types merely happen to share a column. Two types
that both have a `created-at` do not have an interface — they have a **shared
property**. The test: does anything need to treat these types as
interchangeable? If not, an interface is indirection nobody reads.

### Value type or shared property?

- **Value type** — the CONSTRAINT travels. `:iban` is a `:string` that must look
  like an IBAN, wherever it turns up. Pure authoring sugar: it is resolved and
  spliced away at read time, so the live schema sees the base type plus the
  constraints.
- **Shared property** — the COLUMN travels. `:created-at` is one property with
  one meaning, referenced by name from any number of types.

