## 4. What a good ontology looks like

### 4.1 The layer rule — the one that matters most

    source → raw dataset → clean dataset → SHAPING hop → object type

Never bind a source straight to an object type. Land it as a dataset, curate it
into a second dataset, and shape THAT into the type with a cheap deterministic
transform (`:sql`, no egress, no model).

Why the extra hop earns its keep: the shaping hop is free to re-run when the
ontology's shape changes, without touching the expensive extract. Bind the ERP
to the type directly and every ontology edit re-pays the ingest.

Consequence: **the object type carries business vocabulary, not ETL columns.**
`_ingested_at`, batch ids and raw un-normalised strings stay in the dataset,
where they remain available for debugging and are simply not selected.

### 4.2 A type is a noun, not a table

If your object type has the same name and the same 47 columns as an ERP table,
you have imported a table, not modelled a domain. Ask what the business calls
it, what identifies one of them, and what somebody would want to traverse to.

### 4.3 Identity is a decision

An object type's PRIMARY KEY lives on the producing transform, not on the type.
How a key is minted is the most consequential thing you will decide here,
because changing it later does not overwrite the old rows — it mints NEW ones
beside them. See §7.

### 4.4 Consumers join, they do not group

Model the edge as a link and let readers traverse it. Filtering a child set by
*the parent resolves* makes an unreferenced row invisible instead of plausible.
Skipping this once left 661 of 1415 invoice lines orphaned for months, surfacing
as one invoice line shown four times with a fourfold sum.

### 4.5 Say who it is for

`:symbol`, `:name`, `:plural`, `:title` (the property that names an INSTANCE),
`:description`, `:groups`. Not decoration — this is what every application,
search box and object view shows a human. A type with none of them is a type
nobody can find.

### 4.6 Status from day one

Everything starts `:experimental`. Promote to `:active` when an application
depends on it; that promotion is what turns on the protection in §6.

---

## 5. Anti-patterns, and what each one costs

| anti-pattern | the cost |
|---|---|
| **Kitchen sink** — ETL columns exposed as properties | every consumer sees plumbing, and the type stops being a domain concept |
| **Source bound to an object type** | raw rows land in the ontology with no curated layer; every reshape re-pays the ingest |
| **Renaming an `:active` type** | the RID survives, so nothing looks broken from inside — while every consumer holding the NAME breaks. Refused; see §6 |
| **Grouping instead of joining** | orphans become invisible rather than wrong (§4.4) |
| **Changing key derivation without a version bump** | the old rows stay and consumers see both (§7) |
| **Declaring nothing** | an `.ent` with no types is a request to delete everything. Refused, with its own message |
| **Pushing from a stale checkout** | conflicts and phantom errors that have nothing to do with your edit |
| **Umlauts that do not round-trip** | `:anhänge` becomes the column `anhaenge` — `sutra.parser.spec/attr->col` folds ä→ae, ö→oe, ü→ue, ß→ss and `-`→`_`. Emit `anhänge` from a transform while the schema expects `anhaenge` and you get nil rows, silently |

