## 6. Lifecycle and retirement

There is **no** `:retire` section. Retirement is a STATE on the resource, and
the removal is that resource's ABSENCE from this tree.

The rule applies identically at every door — `projection push`, the CLI
`delete` verb, the HTTP API:

> An `:active` or `:endorsed` resource cannot be deleted, and its NAME cannot be
> changed. It has to be `:experimental` or `:deprecated` first.

So retiring something is two ordinary edits to this file:

```clojure
;; 1. announce it ON the thing, where a consumer can see it
:ereignis-partner {…
  :status      :deprecated
  :deprecation {:reason      "superseded by :ereignis-geschaeftspartner"
                :deadline    "2026-09-01"
                :replacement :ereignis-geschaeftspartner}}

;; 2. later: leave it out. That is the removal.
```

**Renaming is the same act in disguise** — a drop plus an add — so it obeys the
same rule. To rename something live: stand the new name up beside it, deprecate
the old one with `:replacement` pointing at the new, move the consumers over,
then drop the old. Three commits, each green on its own.

An unpromoted type needs none of this: omit it and it goes, and the push reports
what it retired.

**The rule is the same for every kind a tree declares.** Here: object and link
types, interfaces, actions, object-type groups, object sets, agents, sources,
declared (`:kind :standalone`) datasets and the object views in `object-views/`.
In a code repository: its own transforms, functions, pipelines and apps. Each
carries `:status`/`:deprecation` (an unmarked one counts as `:experimental`),
and leaving it out removes it — refused, with nothing written, while it is
`:active`/`:endorsed` or something that stays still points at it. Apps and
document templates carry `:draft`/`:published` instead, which is no lifecycle:
leaving one out removes it.

**Taking an object type apart: the ontology first, the code after** (as in
links go before the type; a link you keep that still names it is refused. Only
USERS block the drop: actions, apps and views, links of other types, `:derived`
properties of other types, and transforms that READ the type. A transform that
only FEEDS it (its `:output`, primary or `:supplement`) is no blocker: it stays
orphaned — every build skips it with a notice — until its code repository
leaves it out. The layer rule avoids even that coupling: a transform writes a
dataset and the type names it via `:backing`.

**See it before you push.** `projection push <text> --preview` plans exactly
like the push and lists every removal with its status, and every refusal. Not removed by omission, because no tree is authoritative for them:
value types, shared properties, collections, workspaces and minted datasets (a
transform's output). Delete those with the verb.

